// Class inheritance, and the mapping and query features that only exist because of it.
//
// The other 210 classes have no supertype at all. That absence hid three things: mapping
// `extends [setId]`, `inheritance` Operation mappings, and subtype casts in a graph fetch
// (`->subType(@)`) -- the last of which the PARSER corpus covers and the execution corpus
// had never once run. None of them is reachable by writing more mappings over a flat model;
// they need a hierarchy to exist first.
//
// Deliberately a SEPARATE hierarchy rather than retrofitting `extends` onto the trading
// domain. Making products::Equity a subclass of products::Instrument would change what 397
// existing mappings mean and what every existing service returns, which is a large change
// to make in service of a feature nobody has tested yet.
//
// One table backs the whole hierarchy -- the classic single-table-inheritance shape, where
// the subclass columns are null for rows of the other subclass. That is the interesting
// case rather than the tidy one: SUB_COUPON is null for every option and SUB_STRIKE is null
// for every bond, so a subtype cast has to be right about which rows it keeps.
###Pure
Class hier::Instrument
{
   instrumentId: String[1];
   name: String[1];
   assetClass: String[0..1];
}

Class hier::Bond extends hier::Instrument
{
   coupon: Float[0..1];
}

Class hier::Option extends hier::Instrument
{
   strike: Float[0..1];
}

###Relational
Database hier::HierDB
(
   Table HIER_INSTRUMENT
   (
      INSTRUMENT_ID VARCHAR(64) PRIMARY KEY,
      NAME VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
      ASSET_CLASS VARCHAR(32),
      SUB_KIND VARCHAR(16),
      SUB_COUPON DOUBLE,
      SUB_STRIKE DOUBLE,
      ISSUER_ID VARCHAR(64)
   )

   // The discriminator, used as a class-mapping ~filter so each subclass set sees only its
   // own rows. Without it both subclass sets would return every row of the table.
   Filter BondRows(HIER_INSTRUMENT.SUB_KIND = 'BOND')
   Filter OptionRows(HIER_INSTRUMENT.SUB_KIND = 'OPTION')
)

###Mapping
Mapping hier::HierMapping
(
   // The ROOT set. A class mapped by more than one set implementation needs exactly one
   // root, or compilation fails with "mapped by N set implementations and has 0 roots".
   *hier::Instrument[base]: Relational
   {
      ~primaryKey ( [hier::HierDB]HIER_INSTRUMENT.INSTRUMENT_ID )
      ~mainTable [hier::HierDB]HIER_INSTRUMENT
      instrumentId: [hier::HierDB]HIER_INSTRUMENT.INSTRUMENT_ID,
      name: [hier::HierDB]HIER_INSTRUMENT.NAME,
      assetClass: [hier::HierDB]HIER_INSTRUMENT.ASSET_CLASS
   }

   // `extends [base]` inherits the superclass's property mappings, so each subclass set
   // declares ONLY its own column. Directive order is fixed: ~filter comes before everything
   // else, and an extending set carries no ~mainTable of its own.
   hier::Bond[bond] extends [base]: Relational
   {
      ~filter [hier::HierDB]BondRows
      coupon: [hier::HierDB]HIER_INSTRUMENT.SUB_COUPON
   }

   hier::Option[opt] extends [base]: Relational
   {
      ~filter [hier::HierDB]OptionRows
      strike: [hier::HierDB]HIER_INSTRUMENT.SUB_STRIKE
   }
)
